{# Zone telemetry summary - 3.4.

   One row per zone: how many samples it recorded, the min, mean and max of
   temperature, humidity and vibration RMS across them, and how many alerts
   named the zone. Individual samples are never rendered (2.6).

   Every figure comes from derive.zone_rows. Nothing is counted here, and no
   device flag is tested here: which measurement was suppressed, and why, is
   what detect_gaps and derive.py already worked out (5.4, 5.5).

   There is no particulate column (decision 33). Where the device that would
   have filled one was off, the note under the table says so, built from the
   SUBSYSTEM_OFFLINE gaps rather than from a second look at the flag. #}
<section class="section zone-telemetry" id="zone-telemetry">

  <h2>Zone telemetry</h2>

  <p class="lede">
    Every zone the run passed through, with the range its sensors recorded
    there. These are summaries of the run's continuous telemetry, not readings
    taken at a checkpoint.
  </p>

  {% if not derived.zones %}
    <p>No telemetry was recorded for this run.</p>
  {% else %}

    {# Min, mean and max each get a column, grouped under the measurement they
       belong to. Nine numeric columns on one A4 width could not be followed
       across a row, so they are split into the two tables the measurements
       come from: the bme680's environment figures with the zone's alert count,
       and the adxl345's vibration beside it (decision 164). Every row appears
       in both, in the same order, so the two read as one table folded. #}
    {% for title, columns, with_alerts in zone_column_groups %}
      <h3>{{ title }}</h3>

      <table class="data-table zone-table">
        <colgroup>
          <col class="zone-name">
          <col class="zone-count">
          <col class="zone-figure" span="{{ columns | length * 3 }}">
          {% if with_alerts %}<col class="zone-count">{% endif %}
        </colgroup>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th rowspan="2">Zone</th>
            <th rowspan="2" class="figure">Samples</th>
            {% for field, label, unit, places in columns %}
              {# No configured unit prints the measurement name alone, rather than an
                 empty span the stylesheet would still space for (decision 130). #}
              <th colspan="3" class="zone-group zone-measure">{{ label }}{% if unit %} <span class="unit">{{ unit }}</span>{% endif %}</th>
            {% endfor %}
            {% if with_alerts %}<th rowspan="2" class="figure">Alerts</th>{% endif %}
          </tr>
          <tr>
            {% for field, label, unit, places in columns %}
              <th class="figure zone-group zone-subhead">Min</th>
              <th class="figure zone-subhead">Mean</th>
              <th class="figure zone-subhead">Max</th>
            {% endfor %}
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {% for row in derived.zones %}
            <tr>
              {# A sample or an alert can record no zone at all. The row is kept
                 and named, because a table that quietly dropped a run's worth of
                 telemetry would not say it had. #}
              <td>{{ row.zone | show("No zone recorded") }}</td>
              <td class="figure">{{ row.samples }}</td>
              {% for field, label, unit, places in columns %}
                {% set stat = row | attr(field) %}
                {% if stat.used %}
                  <td class="figure zone-group">{{ stat.minimum | measure(places) }}</td>
                  {# The mean is over the samples that carried this value, which
                     is not always every sample the zone recorded. Saying so keeps
                     the Samples column from reading as the figure the mean was
                     taken over. #}
                  <td class="figure">
                    {{- stat.mean | measure(places) -}}
                    {%- if stat.used != row.samples %}
                      <span class="zone-used">{{ stat.used }} of {{ row.samples }}</span>
                    {%- endif -%}
                  </td>
                  <td class="figure">{{ stat.maximum | measure(places) }}</td>
                {% else %}
                  <td colspan="3" class="zone-group zone-absent">
                    <span class="absent">{{ stat | zone_absence(row.samples) }}</span>
                  </td>
                {% endif %}
              {% endfor %}
              {% if with_alerts %}<td class="figure">{{ row.alerts }}</td>{% endif %}
            </tr>
          {% endfor %}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    {% endfor %}

    {# The note follows the scope of the gap behind it. A device off for the
       whole run raises one gap against __run__ and the note is its sentence; a
       device off at some checkpoints raises one each, and the note counts them
       rather than repeating the same sentence seven times (decision 171). By
       construction it is one or the other, never both. #}
    {% if particulate_offline %}
      <p class="table-note">
        <span class="gap-tag">{{ particulate_offline[0].gap_type }}</span>
        Particulate is not summarised here.
        {% if particulate_offline[0].item_id == run_level %}
          {{ particulate_offline[0].detail }}.
        {% else %}
          The device reported offline at {{ particulate_offline | length }}
          {{ "checkpoint" if particulate_offline | length == 1 else "checkpoints" }},
          so what it wrote there is a placeholder rather than a measurement.
        {% endif %}
        No statistics are computed over placeholder values, and each
        checkpoint's own sensor table names the device.
      </p>
    {% endif %}

  {% endif %}

</section>
